WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:10.980
Neurons in our nervous system use electrical
impulses to transmit information

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:12.210
throughout our bodies.

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:18.460
This process is mediated by membrane proteins
called voltage gated ion channels.

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:24.140
Voltage-gated ion channels open and close in reaction to changes in membrane voltage

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:25.120
or membrane potential.

00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:29.680
A cell membrane potential is generated by
concentration gradients of positively charged

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:32.680
sodium and potassium ions across the membrane.

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:40.260
When the neuron is at rest, the ion-channels are closed,

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:45.400
and the overall concentrations of ions and proteins create a higher potential on the outside

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:48.220
and a lower potential on the inside.

00:00:53.840 --> 00:01:00.120
This state is referred to as resting potential
and the membrane is said to be polarized.

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:04.340
To transmit a signal, the neuron creates an action potential

00:01:04.460 --> 00:01:07.920
which travels down the axon.

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:12.340
The action 
potential is mediated by the opening of the

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:17.180
voltage gated sodium channels, which allow sodium to enter the neuron.

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:23.160
This depolarizes the membrane in the local
area.

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:26.700
Neurons then repolarize the membrane in several
ways.

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:31.980
First, voltage-gated sodium channels close
and become inactivated.

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:40.020
Then, to counteract the positive charge of the sodium ions,

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:43.140
potassium channels start to open allowing potassium ions to exit

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:46.320
lowering the voltage across the membrane.

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:52.240
In this way the signal travels along the neuron.

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:56.540
Sodium channels open and trigger each other
to generate action potentials

00:01:56.780 --> 00:02:02.520
and potassium channels prime the neuron for the next signal by allowing potassium to exit.

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:08.460
After the signal is finished, the locations
of ions have been reversed.

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:14.700
Sodium concentration is high on the inside and potassium concentration is high on the outside

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:20.660
The sodium-potassium pump restores the sodium and potassium gradients

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:22.620
making the cell ready to send another signal.

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:30.200
With each pumping cycle, this transport protein
moves 2 potassium ions back into the cell

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:32.780
and 3 sodium ions out of the cell.

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:40.420
Because the pump moves these ions against their concentration gradients

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:42.800
it requires the energy of one ATP molecule for each cycle.

00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:54.180
This molecular machine comprises three chains:
alpha, beta and gamma.

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:57.980
Within the alpha chain, three domains, A,
P, and N, form the core engine of the pump.

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:02.920
The pumping cycle occurs in several steps.

00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:07.700
With ATP bound in the N site and the transmembrane region open into the cell,

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:11.160
the pump binds three sodium ions from the cytoplasm.

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:21.160
This causes the N, P, and A sites to shift
closer together.

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:24.680
With the help of magnesium co-factor, the
ADP is cleaved off and the P-site is phosphorylated

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:26.680
and ADP is cleaved off.

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:35.700
This leads a conformational change that exposes the the sodium ions to the outside.

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:42.980
The sodium ions can then  be released, one after another, into the extracellular space.

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:49.380
In this ion-empty state, the pump allows the entry of 2 potassium ions.

00:03:57.800 --> 00:04:02.020
This causes dephosphorylation of the pump

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:06.120
followed by closing of the extracellular gate and allows the N domain to accept the ATP again.

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:14.860
As a result of ATP binding, the intracellular gate opens, the potassium ions are released into the cytoplasm,

00:04:14.860 --> 00:04:18.040
and the cycle can start
again.

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:31.540
The sodium-potassium pump is a key protein working to maintain the sodium-potassium gradients

00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:36.180
and the intra- and extracellular potential
differences necessary for neuronal signaling.

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:43.120
Roughly a third of the ATP made daily by our
cells is spent to power this molecular machine.

